finding: High: src/extras/multiplayer/proxy.ts:135 (stop at 75-78) discards queued stream bytes on a normal source socket close, before the delayed/stalled direction drains. Independently reproduced with established loopback TCP sockets through createProxy: set downstream.latency=100 (or downstream.stalled=true); server.end(Buffer.alloc(16384,42)); wait 10 ms; client.end(); wait 30 ms; release stall; wait 200 ms. Client receives clean EOF and 0/16384 bytes, with pendingSockets=0; no resetPeer or disposal occurs. The identical no-fault control receives all 16384 bytes. Both FINs complete the upstream leg while its previously read data remains in the downstream pump; source.close invokes stop(), clearing that queue, and the opposite close handler destroys the destination. Graceful EOF must drain the direction's queue before teardown; only reset/error teardown may discard it. Existing proxy traffic tests remain green because they receive every test payload before closing, and the large queued-byte test disposes rather than checks delivery. This violates the stage's retained, lossless TCP stream claim under delay/stall and release. Focused suite, including check-timeouts and check-package: 28 pass; independent large WebSocket-frame integrity and TCP reset controls pass, so those greens do not cover this EOF sequence.
